text:
anna: heads-up — the latest sample app for the esm build fails when deployed as a Next.js edge function. trace points to fetch being undefined in the worker runtime. anyone seen this?

mike: yeah saw that too. summary: our published package expects global fetch in all runtimes, but some edge runtimes (older CF/Verce/Netlify edge) and some Node setups don't provide it. weirdly local node v18 works for some folks.

jordan: problem is we shipped only an ESM bundle that references globalThis.fetch and didn’t provide a graceful fallback. when consumers bundle for edge it strips polyfills.

sam: pattern we use elsewhere: detect global fetch then fallback to undici dynamically. something like:

```
const fetchImpl = globalThis.fetch ?? (await import('undici')).fetch;
export default function callApi(...args) { return fetchImpl(...args); }
```

but that breaks in true edge runtimes where dynamic import of undici will fail or be blocked.

mike: yeah. undici cannot run in Workers/Edge. so we need three concerns: 1) edge runtimes (Workers) — must use global fetch only, 2) node runtimes w/o global fetch — can use undici, 3) bundlers and ESM/CJS consumers — package exports must be clear.

anna: proposals: a) ship an ESM-first distribution that avoids bundling undici, b) add a small CJS shim that requires undici for Node (commonjs consumers can still use it), c) provide a runtime-safe entry that only loads undici when safe, and d) update README + sample apps to show detection patterns. thoughts?

jordan: agree. also add package.json exports like:

```
"exports": {
  "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
  "require": "./dist/index.cjs",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts"
}
```

then index.mjs should be edge-first and avoid conditional require of undici. index.cjs can assume Node and pull undici sync.

sam: for types we should avoid relying on lib:dom for node-only users. propose we ship a small ambient declaration that references standard fetch types when available, plus fallback Request/Response types in our types file to avoid TS errors for node consumers.

mike: good call. also mention Next.js users: if they target edge functions, they need the ESM edge-friendly bundle. Add note about setting "engines" and recommending Node 18+ for server runtimes that expect global fetch.

anna: what about build pipeline changes? we need CI tests that run sample apps against: node (18), node (16 + undici), and a Workers-ish runtime like miniflare or wrangler dev. ci-bot can we add jobs?

ci-bot: added tickets/ci: +job(samples:node18) +job(samples:node16-undici) +job(samples:miniflare). ETA: next deploy window. :white_check_mark:

jordan: one more DX hit — consumers import types from package root (import { RedwoodClient } from 'redwood') and TS picks up dom lib when tsconfig has it. that leaks. make sure our types are independent: include "types": "./dist/index.d.ts" and ship a self-contained .d.ts that declares minimal Fetch API shapes if dom lib missing.

sam: for docs/sample apps, include this snippet as recommended fallback for libraries that might run in node or edge:

```
// safe-fetch.js
export async function getFetch() {
  if (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function') return globalThis.fetch;
  try {
    // dynamic import only in Node-ish environments
    const { fetch } = await import('undici');
    return fetch;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available in this runtime. Use an edge-compatible build or polyfill.');
  }
}
```

and then call getFetch() in init-time code, not at module top-level.

anna: action items: 1) eng - produce dual builds (esm edge-friendly + cjs node-friendly) + typings self-contained (mike/jordan), 2) devex - update sample apps and onboarding docs with the safe-fetch pattern (sam), 3) ci - add the three sample app test jobs (ci-bot). prioritize for this sprint.

mike: will open PR for build scripts and add conditional exports. will also ensure the CJS build requires undici in package.json dependencies, not optional.

jordan: I’ll work on the types shim and a tiny README section explaining "why undici is not for Workers" with examples.

sam: I’ll update the quickstart and the Next.js example to explicitly recommend the ESM edge bundle for middleware/edge functions. include a note on Vercel’s Edge vs Serverless differences.

anna: thanks all. this should reduce confusion in onboarding and fewer bug reports about undefined fetch in sample apps. :tada:

mike: also logging note — if fetch fallback used, emit a debug log so we can surface in telemetry which fallback used. small change but helpful.

ci-bot: created GH issues: #4123 (dual-build & exports), #4124 (typings shim), #4125 (sample-app runtime tests).
